TY  - JOUR
ID  - CWC-64D1F1401DD1
TI  - Morphological responses of a cup coral to environmental gradients
AU  - James J. Bell
PY  - 2002
T2  - Sarsia
DO  - 10.1080/00364820260400825
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1080/00364820260400825
AB  - The morphology of the cup coral Caryophyllia smithii was studied under different regimes of water movement at Lough Hyne, Co. Cork, Ireland (51degrees29'N 9degrees18'W). Size was found to be significantly greater in wave sheltered environments than in wave exposed locations. Also, the size of corals decreased significantly with increasing depth, but only at wave sheltered locations. The smaller size of corals on the open coast (exposed) was considered to be a result of higher mortality from dislodgement of corals by intense wave action. The decrease in size with depth at sheltered environments is considered with respect to the increased energetic demand from sediment removal, reducing energy available for growth. An increased coral height for any given calyx width was found with increasing depth at sheltered sites and was higher at all depths than at the wave exposed site (even though actual heights decreased). Taller corals (for any given calyx size) release corals from competition with encrusting sponges and keep the calyx above the sediment surface. Upright forms also reduce the amount of sediment falling on feeding surfaces. It is considered that this morphological adaptation is mediated through initial high growth rates at undisturbed sites or reduced growth rates at disturbed sites. However, once corals reach >8 mm in height, the growth rates are the same at all sites. The morphological adaptation of juvenile corals may have important consequences with respect to adaptation of adult corals, should their environment change.
