TY  - CPAPER
ID  - CWC-BF9D8CD707ED
TI  - Use of Riserless Mud Recovery for Protection of Cold Water Corals While Drilling in Norwegian Sea
AU  - Macdonald Daniel
PY  - 2016
T2  - SPE International Conference and Exhibition on Health, Safety, Security, Environment, and Social Responsibility
DO  - 10.2118/179283-ms
UR  - https://doi.org/10.2118/179283-ms
AB  - Abstract The riserless mud recovery (RMR) system has been commercially available for 12 years and used on over 240 wells worldwide. There are several different drivers that typically result in the use of the system and it offers many secondary benefits for top hole drilling, regardless of the primary driver. For the well in question it was used to avoid damaging cold water corals through burial with drilling related sediment. This paper details the background behind the decision to use the system, as well as the system's performance and the resulting benefits and drawbacks to be considered when selecting to use it in this way. During a habitat assessment carried out in 2013, significant quantities of sponge and coral structures were identified as being present on the seafloor around the proposed spud location for well 6507/11-11 in the Norwegian Sea. The well was planned to be the first exploration well in the Zumba prospect in PL 591. A visual survey to assess the condition of the coral structures within 500 m of the planned spud location was carried out in 2014. The results of this survey were used to risk assess the extent of any potential damage to the corals resulting from the drilling process on the Zumba exploration well. This process confirmed the likelihood that some of the corals would be subjected to significant harm if the well was drilled as a conventional exploration well using water based drilling fluid. Typically this would involve discharging displaced drilling fluid, cement slurry and drill cuttings to the sea at or nearby the well site. This assessment drove the decision to contain and ship to shore for disposal all the excess drilling fluids and cuttings. The RMR system was used to achieve this cuttings containment solution during the top hole sections. This was a relatively unique use of the system in Norway because the 36" hole section was also drilled using total containment and no cuttings were discharged at sea.
N1  - Evidence tier: A; Access: NO; corpus_id: CWC-BF9D8CD707ED
ER  -

TY  - JOUR
ID  - CWC-CA349EAD234B
TI  - Potential biomarkers of metal toxicity in deep-sea invertebrates – A critical review of the omics data
AU  - Cármen Sousa
AU  - Jin Sun
AU  - Nélia C. Mestre
PY  - 2024
T2  - The Science of The Total Environment
DO  - 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2024.175628
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scitotenv.2024.175628
AB  - Deep-sea mining (DSM) activities are expected to release potentially toxic metal mixtures through the generation of sediment plumes to the marine environment. This may disrupt the normal functioning of biological mechanisms, adversely affecting deep-sea invertebrate organisms. It is thus essential to understand the ecotoxicological effects from these toxic elements in deep-sea organisms and the omics approaches applied to ecotoxicology are seen as promising tools. Here, we provide an overview of the principal biological modifications identified in deep-sea invertebrates when exposed to metals and critically evaluate the current knowledge and discuss which potential biomarkers may be useful after metal exposure. Most of the 50 omics studies on deep-sea invertebrates revised are comparative transcriptomes ( n = 41). Forty-three potential biomarker candidates are highlighted from immune system, 46 from cellular metabolism and 29 from oxidative stress. The processes mostly affected by metal toxicity in deep-sea invertebrates are related to innate immune defense; sulfur, chitin, and catabolic metabolism; antioxidation; and detoxification. We acknowledge the current limitations and future perspectives for their uses and emphasize the need to invest in further ecotoxicological studies using the omics approaches. Potential biomarkers were proposed for Cnidaria (e.g. coral, anemone), Porifera (e.g. sponge), Echinodermata (e.g. sea cucumber, brittle star, sea urchin), Mollusca (e.g. clam, mussel, snail), Arthropoda (e.g. shrimp, crab, amphipod) and Annelida (e.g. tubeworm) based on several Proteomic, Transcriptomic and Metabolomic published studies. • Fifty omics studies on metal-related exposure in deep-sea invertebrates reviewed • 41 biomarkers candidates prevailed in different omics approaches and various species. • 28 potential biomarkers identified from the few studies with metal challenges • Metal toxicity induced changes in immune system, metabolism and oxidative stress. • Validated biomarkers can be helpful to implement future disturbance threshold levels.
N1  - Evidence tier: A; Access: NO; corpus_id: CWC-CA349EAD234B
