TY  - JOUR
ID  - CWC-01AC06FE96E2
TI  - Impacts of sedimentation on coral health and reef ecosystems: A comprehensive review
AU  - Md. Simul Bhuyan
AU  - Maroua Jenzri
AU  - Diponkor Adikari
PY  - 2025
T2  - Marine Pollution Bulletin
DO  - 10.1016/j.marpolbul.2025.118480
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.marpolbul.2025.118480
AB  - Sedimentation is a major yet often underestimated driver of coral reef degradation, affecting coral physiology, reproduction, and ecosystem structure. This review synthesizes findings from 252 peer-reviewed studies (1977-2025) identified through systematic searches using PRISMA guidelines. A bibliometric analysis conducted with VOSviewer (v1.6.17) revealed ten co-authorship clusters, highlighting influential contributors such as Erftemeijer, Paul L.A., and Jones, Ross. The review examines sediment delivery pathways-terrestrial runoff, dredging, and biogenic sources-and their impacts across coral life stages. Dominant affected species include Acropora (27 %), Montipora (18 %), and Porites (16 %), which suffer from reduced photosynthesis, smothering, decreased recruitment, and elevated mortality. Fine sediments impair light penetration, carry toxins, and damage coral tissues. Coral recruits show heightened sensitivity, with sedimentation significantly lowering fertilization, settlement, and survival rates. Although some species display tolerance mechanisms, these are inadequate under chronic stress. The study underscores the urgency for integrated watershed management and adaptive mitigation to bolster coral resilience amid rising anthropogenic and climate stressors.
