TY  - CPAPER
ID  - CWC-69942A71FF7E
TI  - Particle capture and transport in the demosponge Halichondria panicea :Speed talk and poster
AU  - Rachael A. Kealy
AU  - Josephine Goldstein
AU  - Hans Ulrik Riisgård
AU  - Jonathan R. Brewer
AU  - Vita Solovyeva
AU  - Peter Funch
PY  - 2022
UR  - https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/publications/4578672a-740d-4bbc-9973-1174456bd9e0
AB  - Sponges are efficient filter-feeders that encounter various food regimes with changing loads of inedible particles, such as suspended sediment, in their natural habitats. In some demosponges, peristaltic-like contractions of the aquiferous system facilitate removal of inedible material. Other studies have indicated selective feeding on certain types of food particles. However, the detailed mechanisms, cell types and aquiferous structures involved in capture, transport, and removal of different types of particles in sponges are not fully understood. Here we identify and characterize the sponge cell types and morphological structures involved in contractions, particle transport and water flow in the aquiferous system of the marine demosponge Halichondria panicea when exposed to different particle types and concentrations. We describe trigger level concentrations of edible and inedible particles of two distinct size groups (2 and 10 µm) which can reliably induce contractions in explants of H. panicea . We track the same particle types in the aquiferous system and mesohyl of sandwich cultures and cryofractured explants using live-cell imaging and SEM, respectively. Our study shows that edible 2 µm-particles which enter choanocyte chambers are captured and phagocytosed by choanocytes, while inedible particles are removed via apopyles with the water flow through excurrent canals. 10 µm-particles that cannot enter ostia or prosopyles are captured either on the sponge surface or in incurrent canals before they are phagocytosed by exo- or endopinacocytes, respectively. Afterwards they are transferred to amoeboid cells in the mesohyl which digest edible particle after disintegration into smaller fragments and remove inedible particles via exocytosis by endopinacocytes of the excurrent canals. Apart from feeding, possible functions of the observed capture, transport and removal mechanisms are cleaning of the aquiferous system and prevention of clogging of the sponge filter-pump.
N1  - Evidence tier: B; Access: NO; corpus_id: CWC-69942A71FF7E
