TY  - JOUR
ID  - CWC-C1963903542C
TI  - Sediment accumulation on a cold-water carbonate mound at the Southwest Rockall Trough margin
AU  - Furu Mienis
AU  - Cees van der Land
AU  - Henko de Stigter
AU  - M. van de Vorstenbosch
AU  - H. de Haas
AU  - Thomas Richter
AU  - Tj.C.E. van Weering
PY  - 2009
T2  - Marine Geology
DO  - 10.1016/j.margeo.2009.06.014
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.margeo.2009.06.014
AB  - Cold-water corals build isolated and clustered carbonate mound ridges, several kilometres long and wide and up to 380 m high at the Southwest Rockall Trough margin. Photo and video observations as well as coring have shown that mainly the upper mound flanks and summits are covered with a thriving living coral cover and associated fauna. Box cores prove the presence of living coral colonies on top of a thick layer of coral which is to a large extent abraded and bio-eroded, before being buried. The coral framework is filled debris, with sediment from settling pelagic material and parts of the fauna living at the mounds, resulting in a 120 cm thick layer of sediments since 10,800 yr. Component analysis of a piston core from the top of a carbonate mound shows a complete record of cold-comprising many different species living at the mounds. An alternation of skeletal water coral mound facies, and cement dominated intervals was distinguished in the piston core. Stable isotope analysis of planktonic and benthic foraminifera indicates a continuous sedimentation pattern since the Younger Dryas. The deeper and older part of the core, showing mainly intermediate isotope values, is dominated by the presence of large hiatuses, spanning a total of up to 200,000 yr. Hiatuses in the core may reflect periods of non-deposition or which may be linked to glacial-interglacial variability. Environmental conditions probably change at erosion, glacial-interglacial timescales, influencing the local hydrodynamic regime, food supply and sedimentation rate around the carbonate mounds affecting coral growth and therefore carbonate mound development. (C) 2009 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
N1  - Evidence tier: B; Access: NO; corpus_id: CWC-C1963903542C
